  ('accelerator-short-foot-drill-bilateral-supported-stance', 'Short-Foot Drill — bilateral supported stance', '{"slug":"accelerator-short-foot-drill-bilateral-supported-stance","name":"Short-Foot Drill — bilateral supported stance","family":"Single-leg and lower-limb resilience","primaryPhaseKey":"resilience","subrole":"single_leg_balance_foot_ankle_hip_control","slot":"accelerator_resilience_single_leg","cardSummary":"Maintain familiar light active-arch support while limiting unnecessary novelty before the final exposure. Both feet supported, one target arch gently shortens while toes stay long and heel remains grounded; relax…","bestPlacement":"Use in the Accelerator resilience block with the listed dose and recovery. Build control and tissue tolerance without turning the drill into conditioning or pre-fatiguing later work.","description":"Maintain familiar light active-arch support while limiting unnecessary novelty before the final exposure. Both feet supported, one target arch gently shortens while toes stay long and heel remains grounded; relax between holds. Use seated execution instead if standing causes toe grip. Do not increase hold force to create fatigue.","coachLanguage":"This card represents the exact Accelerator-listed identity “Short-Foot Drill — bilateral supported stance.” Preserve its declared stance, direction, support, landing or receiving condition, and equipment boundary.","athleteLanguage":"Move with control and stop before the position changes.","participantStructure":"individual","tenets":[{"key":"body_control","weight":5}],"methodologies":[{"key":"balance_stability","weight":5}],"physiology":[{"key":"control_stability","weight":5}],"patterns":[{"key":"brace","weight":5}],"equipment":[{"key":"none","weight":5}],"body_regions":[{"key":"ankle","weight":5}],"whyItWorks":"Maintain familiar light active-arch support while limiting unnecessary novelty before the final exposure. Both feet supported, one target arch gently shortens while toes stay